import{_ as t,o,c as s,ao as i}from"./chunks/framework.CSb4BAU4.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Two-room process","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"process.md","filePath":"process.md","lastUpdated":null}'),r={name:"process.md"};function a(c,e,d,n,l,p){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="Two-room-process" tabindex="-1">Two-room process <a class="header-anchor" href="#Two-room-process" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two-room process {#Two-room-process}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>PureXC is MIT-licensed; Libxc, the reference its tests compare with, is MPL-2.0. So that no Libxc code reaches PureXC, every functional passes through two separate rooms, and the record of both is kept in the repository under <code>PureXC/specs/</code>.</p><p><strong>Room 1, the spec writer</strong>, reads the published papers first, then Libxc&#39;s public documentation, its public API and its outputs, and opens Libxc&#39;s source only for what those leave open (which variant of a formula, a misprint, a constant the papers round, how a threshold is applied). Every fact taken from Libxc is marked &quot;checked against Libxc&quot;. A spec holds facts only, equations and numbers, never code. There is one spec per functional, <code>PureXC/specs/&lt;family&gt;/&lt;libxc_id&gt;.md</code>:</p><ol><li><p>identifier, family, kind, spin cases;</p></li><li><p>references (DOI, equation numbers);</p></li><li><p>variables and units;</p></li><li><p>equations, each with its paper and equation number;</p></li><li><p>constants, each with its value, its source, and &quot;paper&quot; or &quot;checked against Libxc&quot;;</p></li><li><p>building blocks (other specs);</p></li><li><p>domain, thresholds and limits, with the functional&#39;s PureXC threshold rule;</p></li><li><p>known misprints or ambiguities, and how each is resolved;</p></li><li><p>the Libxc oracle identifier and the parameter values its public API reports.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Room 2, the implementer and the reviewer</strong>, read the specs, the papers and the repository, and never Libxc&#39;s C or Maple source, any file of the Julia packages <code>Libxc</code> and <code>Libxc_jll</code>, or any page that shows Libxc source. The reviewer holds the same restriction as the implementer and checks the code against the spec.</p><p>The prompts of the three roles are <code>PureXC/specs/prompts/spec_writer.md</code>, <code>implementer.md</code> and <code>reviewer.md</code>. The specs of the shipped functionals:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">functional</th><th style="text-align:right;">spec</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>lda_x</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>PureXC/specs/lda/lda_x.md</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>lda_c_pw</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>PureXC/specs/lda/lda_c_pw.md</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>lda_c_pw_mod</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>PureXC/specs/lda/lda_c_pw_mod.md</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>pbe_x</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>PureXC/specs/gga/gga_x_pbe.md</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>pbe_c</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>PureXC/specs/gga/gga_c_pbe.md</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Libxc itself is called only through Libxc.jl&#39;s <code>Libxc.Functional</code>, <code>evaluate</code> and <code>evaluate!</code>, and only in the comparison tests (<code>PureXC/test/libxc/</code>) and the speed bench (<code>PureXC/bench/</code>), whose environments are the only ones that hold it. The item &quot;Libxc is not a dependency&quot; checks that neither the package nor its main test environment names Libxc or Libxc_jll.</p>',8)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",a]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
